// WHY: the four typed node-id newtypes, hoisted out of arena.av so BOTH
// halves of the node model can name them: arena.av (the id-keyed stores)
// and ast.av (container structs and, as the B-final flip proceeds,
// variant payloads carry id fields). ids.av imports nothing, so ast.av
// gaining `use core.{BuildName, ExprId, ...}` introduces no core-file cycle — the
// same reason AnyNode lives in ast.av rather than arena.av.
//
// Typed ids are OPAQUE HANDLES (compare / look up, never inspect):
// typeck keeps them nominally distinct — passing a StmtId where an
// ExprId is wanted is a type error (arena_test.av) — and each side-table
// is keyed by its own id kind. Construct `StmtId(i)`, peel with `.raw()`
// inside the node model only — COMPILER-ENFORCED: @opaque gates `.raw()`
// to the declaring module (F3106 anywhere else; `core/` is one module, so
// arena.av and side_table.av are covered), and store boundaries that must
// round-trip an id through i64 go through arena.av's expr_id_store_key —
// the handle representation can change without hunting stray inspections.

/// Index into the Expr arena — nominally distinct from StmtId/PatId/TypeId/int.
@opaque
export type ExprId = int
/// Index into the Stmt arena.
@opaque
export type StmtId = int
/// Index into the Pattern arena.
@opaque
export type PatId = int
/// Index into the ValueType arena.
@opaque
export type TypeId = int

/// A grammar rule's name — the `start` argument of the executor seams.
///
/// t-y2i7.3 section 14. The seams took a bare `string`, so any string was a
/// valid rule name and a rule name was interchangeable with any other string.
/// It is OPTIONAL at the seam (`RuleName?`) rather than carrying a `""`
/// sentinel: an empty rule name is a different thing from "no rule given", and
/// conflating them is what broke the first attempt at this (312 grammar specs).
/// `null` now means "use the grammar's first rule".
export type RuleName = string

/// A lowering module's dotted path — `features.tuples.lowering`, the value
/// `bind_rows` stamps across every row of a feature's manifest.
///
/// t-y2i7.3 section 14. It was a bare `string`, which is what the ticket's
/// motivating example turns on: `b.module != "features.grammar"` in emit.av
/// compares against a hand-written literal, and a typo there is silent.
///
/// REQUIRED, not optional, with `ModulePath("")` as the not-yet-stamped value
/// that `bind_row` sets and `bind_rows` overwrites. The optional shape reads
/// better and is what `RuleName` uses, but it cannot be used here: `${opt}`
/// interpolates an optional as a raw POINTER ADDRESS, and ~15 lang-definition
/// tests interpolate this field directly. Measured, not assumed — a required
/// newtype interpolates as its underlying string.
export type ModulePath = string

/// A language feature's declared name — `if_stmt`, `calls`: the `name` a
/// `LanguageFeature` gives itself, and the key every manifest defect is grouped
/// under.
///
/// t-y2i7.3 section 14. It was a bare `string`, so a feature name, a build
/// name, a rule name and a module path were one type and interchangeable by
/// accident.
export type FeatureName = string

/// A build's name — `MkIfExpr`: the label a grammar sequence attaches to the
/// lowering that constructs its AST node.
///
/// It was a bare `string` on BOTH sides of a seam that only ever agreed by
/// convention: the grammar writes `-> MkIfExpr` as DSL text, the feature's
/// manifest writes `bind_row(BuildName("MkIfExpr"), …)` as an Avra literal, and
/// nothing typed the correspondence.
export type BuildName = string

/// A build name displays as the string it wraps.
///
/// Needed because typeck asks for a `display` method whenever an IMPORTED
/// newtype is interpolated: it arrives with its Newtype kind erased to
/// `.Struct(name)`, and typeck does not consult the registry that would tell it
/// apart from a real struct. Codegen already unwraps newtypes for display on
/// its own (#1345); teaching typeck the same is a separate change. This impl is
/// explicit, costs nothing, and stays correct either way.
impl BuildName {
    fn display(self) -> string { self.raw() }
}

